/target
/fuzz/target
/fuzz/artifacts
# `cargo fuzz coverage` output: multi-MB .profraw/.profdata build artifacts.
/fuzz/coverage
# Keep a hand-curated committed seed corpus (seed-*); ignore libFuzzer's hash-named generated
# entries AND the registry-derived seeds from gen-fuzz-corpus (gen-*).
/fuzz/corpus/parse/*
!/fuzz/corpus/parse/seed-*
# Registry-derived fuzz dictionary (regenerated by gen-fuzz-corpus, never committed).
/fuzz/dict
.claude
docs/src/commands/
docs/src/includes/command-count.md
docs/src/version-footer.js
docs/book/
.pstyle_dump.txt

# Locally-generated corpora for the property targets. The nightly accumulates these through the
# actions cache, one entry per target, so committing a few hundred hash-named inputs from a laptop
# run adds noise without adding coverage.
fuzz/corpus/equivalence/
fuzz/corpus/hook_envelope/
fuzz/corpus/explain_render/
fuzz/corpus/suggest_roundtrip/
fuzz/corpus/level_monotonic/
fuzz/corpus/config_load/
fuzz/corpus/setup_merge/
fuzz/corpus/path_admit/
fuzz/corpus/gate_prefilter/
fuzz/artifacts/

# Playwright MCP session artifacts: page snapshots, console logs, screenshots.
.playwright-mcp/
COMMANDS.md.tmp
COMMANDS.md.check
# `safe-chains --suggest` WRITES a repo config as a side effect, so testing that path inside this
# repo leaves one behind. Under jj an untracked file is snapshotted by the next command, so it
# lands in a commit with nobody running an `add` — which happened while reviewing this stack.
# A real `.safe-chains.toml` for this project would need `git add -f`, deliberately.
.safe-chains.toml
